  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "v1.3.2:\n\t\t\t- Added the EventFlux icon to the package and the README. No code changes\n\n\t\t\tv1.3.1:\n\t\t\t- Depends on EventFlux 2.1.1 (maintenance release, same API and behaviour as 2.1.0). No other changes\n\n\t\t\tv1.3.0:\n\t\t\t- Publishing reuses channels from a small pool instead of opening and closing one per message (about 5x faster sequential publishing, 2-3x with publisher confirms)\n\t\t\t- Unsubscribe keeps the event queue and the messages waiting in it (unbinds it and cancels its consumer); the previous deletion is available with RabbitFlowOptions.DeleteQueueOnUnsubscribe\n\t\t\t- Graceful shutdown: consumers stop first and messages being handled finish and are acknowledged before the channel closes, so they are no longer handled twice after a restart. Added RabbitFlowOptions.ShutdownTimeout (30 s) for disposal without AutoSubscribe\n\t\t\t- EventBusSubscriptionsManager is thread safe: concurrent subscriptions no longer make every message of an event fail, handler lists are snapshots, and an unknown event returns no handlers instead of throwing\n\t\t\t- A stopped broker no longer resumes consuming after a channel is closed\n\t\t\t- Message format unchanged; no API removed\n\n\t\t\tv1.2.0:\n\t\t\t- Connecting no longer blocks a thread: added IPersistenceConnection.TryConnectAsync(CancellationToken) (default implementation calls TryConnect), used by publish and subscribe. The token passed to PublishAsync also cancels waiting for the broker, and disposing the connection stops a reconnect in progress\n\t\t\t- Fixed message loss after a broker restart without automatic recovery: exchanges, queues, bindings and dead-letter queues are declared again before consumers restart\n\t\t\t- Fixed consumers never restarting after the broker closed the consumer channel while automatic recovery was enabled (the default)\n\t\t\t- Consumer restart is retried with back-off while the broker is unreachable instead of giving up after one failure\n\t\t\t- Added RabbitFlowOptions.MaxDeliveryAttempts and RedeliveryDelay (opt-in): a failing message is retried a limited number of times, optionally after a delay, then moved to the dead-letter queue. Without them nothing changes\n\t\t\t- Added RabbitFlowOptions.PublisherConfirms (opt-in): PublishAsync completes after the broker confirms the message; rejected messages are retried and then throw, events without a bound queue are logged\n\t\t\t- Fixed message loss in DeadLetter mode: dead-letter publishes are confirmed by the broker before the original message is acknowledged, and requeued if they cannot be routed\n\t\t\t- Added IPersistenceConnection.CreateChannelAsync(CreateChannelOptions, CancellationToken) with a default implementation\n\t\t\t- No breaking changes; message format unchanged\n\n\t\t\tv1.1.0:\n\t\t\t- Migrated to EventFlux 2.1.0 (requires EventFlux >= 2.1.0). Incoming messages are dispatched with IEventBus.PublishAsync(IEventRequest, CancellationToken); the MissingMethodException that made 1.0.3 drop every message under EventFlux 2.x is gone\n\t\t\t- Event handlers now use the EventFlux 2.x signature Handle(TEvent, CancellationToken); the token is the delivery's cancellation token\n\t\t\t- Targets net8.0, net9.0 and net10.0 (net6.0 and net7.0 were dropped because EventFlux 2.x does not support them). Microsoft.Extensions.* dependencies follow the major version of each target, so net8 apps no longer hit CS1705 or assembly load errors\n\t\t\t- New failure semantics: a message whose handler throws is no longer acknowledged. By default it is rejected and requeued (RabbitFlowOptions.OnFailure = Requeue); OnFailure = DeadLetter moves it to a dead-letter queue instead. Messages are never lost on handler failure\n\t\t\t- IEventBroker, IPersistenceConnection and IEventBusSubscriptionsManager are singletons, so subscriptions live until the host stops instead of ending with the scope that created them\n\t\t\t- Added RabbitFlowOptions and an AddEventFluxRabbitFlow overload to configure them (retry count, failure handling, dead-letter names, prefetch, auto subscription)\n\t\t\t- Added RabbitFlowOptions.AutoSubscribe, which subscribes every handler in the scanned assembly when the host starts, and made IServiceProvider.UseEventFluxRabbitFlow public\n\t\t\t- Added CancellationToken overloads to IEventBroker.PublishAsync\n\t\t\t- Subscribing the same handler twice, or to a second exchange, no longer throws\n\t\t\t- Publish retries now cover the whole publish call and keep the original exception\n\t\t\t- Connection recovery: with automatic recovery enabled (the default) the client's own recovery is no longer interrupted by a second connection; with it disabled the connection is replaced, the old one released and consumers are restarted. Blocked connections and callback exceptions no longer trigger a reconnect, and concurrent connect calls open a single connection\n\t\t\t- Publishing while the connection is recovering is retried instead of failing immediately\n\t\t\t- Removed the \"throw-fake-exception\" test hook from the consumer\n\t\t\t- Log messages are in English and use structured templates\n\t\t\t- Message format is unchanged: same JSON bodies, routing key = event type name, same exchange and queue names",
